00:52:06.655 --> 00:52:15.590
what four ninety is. So the very simple version is it's referred to as public act four ninety, which is the state, the state bill

00:52:15.590 --> 00:52:18.550
that it was given when it was passed about fifty years ago.

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:21.190
It basically empowers towns

00:52:21.430 --> 00:52:22.950
to provide a

00:52:24.470 --> 00:52:28.965
tax incentive, a local property tax incentive to keep property

00:52:29.125 --> 00:52:30.165
undeveloped.

00:52:30.165 --> 00:52:31.925
It's not a permanent designation,

00:52:32.325 --> 00:52:34.165
but there are three categories

00:52:34.645 --> 00:52:35.525
farmland,

00:52:35.605 --> 00:52:37.685
forest land, and open space.

00:52:38.165 --> 00:52:45.040
Farmland and forest land each have acreage minimums that are set by the state. So if you've got a 50 acre

00:52:45.040 --> 00:52:46.320
tract of forest,

00:52:46.560 --> 00:53:00.585
you can enroll that through your local assessor for a period of ten years where you say, promise I will not develop this forest, will not chop on the trees, will not put up houses, but not built there. Then the town basically says thank you.

00:53:01.385 --> 00:53:04.345
Sign a little contract for ten years and you get a preferred

00:53:04.505 --> 00:53:09.385
>> tax tax rate. So it's not it's not taxed as a development model. It's not prohibited

00:53:09.385 --> 00:53:10.905
for report. Correct.

00:53:11.385 --> 00:53:13.640
Yes. That's you should be. Similar

00:53:17.080 --> 00:53:18.920
>> requirement whether

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:23.000
it's a state standard. Open space is sort of defined locally.

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:38.465
Each town can kind of define what its open space tax advantage parameters are. A lot of towns say basically if you live in a in our Eighties zone or our sixties zone, that's 60,000 square feet. If you have more than 60,000 square feet, so you've got four acres, you could set aside,

00:53:39.060 --> 00:53:43.540
you know, two and a half of that as open space and get a tax advantage on it.

